## 2. Gauge Text Offset Parity — RESOLVED [CYD-AA-GAUGE]

> **CORRECTED 2026-08-14 — this section was DRIFTED and actively dangerous.**
> It previously asserted that C++ and Python diverged (C++ `0.58`/`0.78` vs Python
> `0.32`/`0.50`) and instructed a builder to edit `gauge.cpp` to `0.32f`/`0.50f`.
> **Both renderers now use `0.58`/`0.78` — they are already identical.** A builder
> following the previous text would have BROKEN the parity that exists. The divergence
> was resolved in the opposite direction from the one this spec prescribed: the C++
> (embedded) values became canonical and the desktop renderer moved to match, not the
> reverse. Drift baseline: `[DEC-SPEC-DRIFT-BASELINE-CYD-2026-08]`.

### 2.1 The Requirement [CYD-AA-GAUGE-REQ]

The two renderers must place gauge value text and label text at the **same** fraction of
`outer_r` below the arc center. A gauge drawn on the CYD and the same gauge drawn by the
desktop Skia renderer must be comparable pixel-for-pixel; a divergent text offset makes
the desktop crosscheck PNG useless as a reference, which is the mechanism [CYD-AA-BUILD]
§4.2 depends on for visual validation.

### 2.2 Achieved State [CYD-AA-GAUGE-ACHIEVED]

Parity holds. Measured 2026-08-14 at commit `153b050`:

| Offset | C++ (`cyd/src/gauge.cpp`) | Python (`python/composeio/render/widgets/gauge.py`) |
|--------|---------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| Value text Y | `cy + outer_r * 0.58f` — `gauge.cpp:308` | `cy + outer_r * 0.58` — `gauge.py:424` |
| Label text Y | `cy + outer_r * 0.78f` — `gauge.cpp:314` | `cy + outer_r * 0.78` — `gauge.py:430` |

The surrounding font sizing agrees on the same terms and is part of the same parity
surface: value font `outer_r * 0.22` bold (`gauge.cpp:306` / `gauge.py:422`), label font
`outer_r * 0.13` regular (`gauge.cpp:312` / `gauge.py:428`), hub radius `outer_r * 0.06`
(`gauge.cpp:297` / `gauge.py:416`), inner radius `outer_r * 0.72` (`gauge.cpp:199` /
`gauge.py:345`).

**Why `0.58`/`0.78` and not the original `0.32`/`0.50`:** the larger offsets were adopted
during Phase 1b to clear the needle hub on small (115x115) gauges, where `0.32` put the
value text on top of the hub. That constraint is real on the embedded tier and harmless on
the desktop, so the embedded value won and the desktop renderer adopted it.

### 2.3 Compliance [CYD-AA-GAUGE-COMPLIANCE]

### [CYD-AA-C6] Gauge value text Y offset is identical in both renderers (`0.58 * outer_r`) — SATISFIED
### [CYD-AA-C7] Gauge label text Y offset is identical in both renderers (`0.78 * outer_r`) — SATISFIED
### [CYD-AA-C9] Any future change to a gauge geometry constant is made in BOTH `gauge.cpp` and `gauge.py` in the same change, whichever tier motivates it

