## 3. Font Atlas — Gauge Label Pre-rendering [CYD-AA-FONT]

### 3.1 Current State [CYD-AA-FONT-CURRENT]

> **CORRECTED 2026-08-14 — size list drifted.** This paragraph previously said the atlas
> contained "sizes 10, 12, 14, 16px" and knew of only one weight. The atlas has since
> grown three small sizes, a bold family, and a separate COMMP family. Measured below at
> commit `153b050`. The requirement in §3.2 and its compliance criterion were unaffected
> and are unchanged. Drift baseline: `[DEC-SPEC-DRIFT-BASELINE-CYD-2026-08]`.

The atlas generator (`fonts/generate_atlas.py`) uses FreeType with 8-bit grayscale
antialiasing, and now emits three families:

| Family | Sizes (px) | Source of truth |
|--------|-----------|-----------------|
| Regular | 6, 7, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16 | `fonts/generate_atlas.py:438-439` (`--sizes` default) |
| Bold | 6, 7, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16 | same `--sizes` list, rendered from the bold face |
| COMMP (Common Pixel) | 12, 14, 16, 20, 24 | `fonts/generate_atlas.py:446-447` (`--commp-sizes` default) |

The generated header restates the list it was built from (`src/font_atlas.h:6-9`), so the
atlas is self-describing — read it there rather than trusting this table if the two ever
disagree. Selection at draw time goes through `font_atlas_find_styled(commp, bold, size)`
(`canvas_esp32.cpp:1867,1898`), not the size-only `font_atlas_find()`
(`font_atlas.h:4752`); weight and family are part of the lookup key.

Gauge text selects its size from the widget's radius, so the sizes actually demanded are
continuous, not from a fixed list: value text at `outer_r * 0.22` bold and label text at
`outer_r * 0.13` regular (`gauge.cpp:306,312`). On a 115x115 gauge (`outer_r` ~57.5) that
is ~12.6px bold and ~7.5px regular — both inside the generated range, which is why the
6/7/8px additions exist.

### 3.2 Requirement [CYD-AA-FONT-REQ]

Verify that the gauge subtitle text ("Coolant Temp", "Oil Pressure") renders cleanly at its target font size. If the gauge uses a size not in the atlas (e.g., 8px or 9px), `font_atlas_find()` returns the nearest match (10px) and the text gets nearest-neighbor scaled, producing artifacts.

If any gauge text renders at a size not in the atlas, add that size to the atlas generation. Run `python3 fonts/generate_atlas.py` with the updated size list and regenerate `src/font_atlas.h`.

### 3.3 Compliance [CYD-AA-FONT-COMPLIANCE]

### [CYD-AA-C8] All text visible in the dashboard demo renders from a pre-generated atlas at its exact target size (no nearest-neighbor scaling between sizes)

